"""Translate the whole Catalog of Prompts into Mexican Spanish.

Angela's Spanish edition shows the catalog cards to a Mexican developer, but
their CONTENT was still 100% English - 113 prompts, ~280k characters, not one
accented character. This migration replaces every ``promptContent`` with its
Spanish translation.

WHAT THIS MIGRATION DOES **NOT** TOUCH
--------------------------------------
``idPrompt``, ``promptName``, ``category``, ``sort_rank`` and ``hidden`` are
left exactly as they are. The catalog's contiguity + ordering contract
(``agent/test_prompt_catalog_contiguous.py``) therefore still holds: same ids,
same sections, same order. Only the human-readable body changes.

WHY THE TEXT COULD NOT SIMPLY BE TRANSLATED
-------------------------------------------
The GUI decides which toolbar checkboxes a card ticks by READING THE PROMPT
TEXT (``tools_dialog.js::classifyPromptModes``). Two rules there were
English-only, so a naive translation silently mis-set the toggles:

  * the "do NOT use <tool>" scrubber - a Spanish "no uses acp_spawn" would have
    been read as REQUIRING acpx;
  * the Step-by-Step detector - it matched English intent words only.

``tools_dialog.js`` was made BILINGUAL first (it now also accepts
"no uses / nunca uses / no utilices" and the hyphenated Spanish checkbox name
"Paso-a-Paso" beside a Spanish verb). Every translation below was then verified
mechanically: each Spanish prompt must produce the SAME classifier verdict as
its English original, and must preserve every functional token byte-exact -
tool names (chat_agent_* / acp_*), agent display names (STM32er, PDFer,
Kyber-KeyGen, ...), {{runtime}} identifiers, [[user input]] blocks and
END-RESPONSE. 113/113 passed before this file was generated.

The same content is also kept, human-readable, in ``agent/i18n/prompts_es.json``
(and the English originals in ``agent/i18n/prompts_en_backup.json``) so an
advanced user can audit or edit the wording without reading a migration.
"""

from django.db import migrations


